@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the second person who stays outside the state
#
# One generalization, formed from three attested prerequisites in three
# transmissions between which no route has been proposed: the Greenland seiðr
# of Eiríks saga rauða, the Chukchee performance recorded by Bogoras, and the
# self-remembering of the Fourth Way.
#
# It is filed on its own rather than with the cautions because its subject is
# not a risk but a division of labour, and because the counter-search found the
# opposite requirement standing in the same holding. Two transmissions here
# demand solitude for their operations in as many words. What decides between
# the two groups is not how strange the operation is but whether the operator
# can check his own performance while he is performing it.
#

pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The second participant who does not enter the state"@en, "Der zweite Beteiligte, der den Zustand nicht betritt"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission has its practitioner enter, during the operation, a condition in which he can no longer check whether the operation is going as it should, and states that the operation does not come off without a second person, that person stays outside the condition. Transmissions that take a practitioner into such a condition do not always make that statement: they may name nobody, a companion who enters the same condition and is required for the practitioner's safety, persons who keep him from entering it, or an interpreter consulted after the operation. Where the transmission instead binds the effect to something the operator can check on himself while he does it — an exact wording, a count, a prescribed hour, or a felt state he is told to hold — it names no such person and may require him to be alone."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:NorseSeidrPresupposesVardlokkur, pmc:ChukcheePresupposesAnsweringCalls, pmc:AwakeningPresupposesAnotherWhoDoesNotSleep ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The three witnesses, each stating the condition in its own way and each stating a consequence. In Greenland the seeress asks for women who know the lore called Varðlokkur, none is found, the household is searched, and after the song is finally sung by a Christian woman who says she is no practitioner at all, the seeress reports that the spirits had before this wished to part from them and give them no obedience, and that things now lie plain that were hidden. Among the Chukchee the recorder puts it as an incapacity: without one who gives answering calls, a shaman considers himself unable to perform his calling in a proper way, and novices induce a brother or a sister to respond. In the Fourth Way material the impossibility is argued rather than reported: a person cannot awaken alone, and an agreement among twenty to wake one another is still insufficient, because all twenty may fall asleep at the same time and dream that they are waking."@en ;
    skos:note "What the three have in common is the reason, and in the third case the source states it: the sleeper cannot detect his own lapse, so the detection has to come from outside him. The same holds in the other two without being spelled out, because in both the practitioner is by then in the state the operation consists in. That is why the second person is not an assistant in the ordinary sense — nothing in any of the three has him fetch, hold or prepare anything. His office is to remain in the condition the practitioner has left."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, and it came back full, which is why the statement has a second clause. The Ars notoria requires the orations to be pronounced alone, secretly, and in a voice barely audible to the speaker; the great Paris papyrus states that its rite is for one person alone, who is to raise himself, and that the mysteries may be handed on by a father to a son and to no one else. Both are genuine opposites of the first clause and neither is a counterexample to it, because in both the effect is bound to something the operator monitors himself: the exactness of the wording, the number of repetitions, the day of the moon and the hour. Where self-checking is possible the transmissions ask for solitude; where it is not, they ask for a second person. The statement was rewritten to say that, instead of the first clause alone, which the holding would have refuted on the day it was written."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that has its practitioner enter such a condition, states a means of checking the operation that only the practitioner himself could apply, and names no second person — or, more sharply, by one that requires solitude for an operation in which the practitioner is said to lose command of himself. The second shape is the one to look for first, and the corpora most likely to hold it are the solitary vigil and the fast: the Lakota hanblapi, the incubation literature, and the desert material, all of which this holding already carries and none of which has been asked this question."@en ;
    skos:note "Weakened, without being refuted, if the three turn out to be reachable from one another. No route between them has been proposed and the two older ones lie far apart; the third is modern and its recorder was a man of letters in Saint Petersburg and Constantinople, so a route from the ethnographic literature of his own decades to his account cannot be excluded and has not been examined. That is the weakest of the three legs and the statement rests on it for the argued form of the claim rather than for the claim itself."@en ;
    skos:note "Rewritten on 2026-10-15 after the protocol at pmp:OneWhoStaysOutsideExamination. The first clause read until then: it names a second person who stays outside that condition and makes the outcome depend on him, and it states that without that person the operation does not come off. The protocol found the Neoplatonic dream divination naming nobody, the Hasidic seclusion requiring a companion inside the same condition and for safety, and the Lakota quest placing its outside person after the operation; only the three witnesses of this node state the clause as first worded, so the clause is now conditional on the transmission saying that the operation fails without the second person, and the other positions are recorded as observed rather than predicted. The second clause stood, and its list of self-checks was widened by Keter Shem Tov, which requires solitude where the practitioner is to hold his own felt position in the upper worlds."@en ;
    skos:note "What it does not say. It says nothing about whether these operations produce anything, and nothing about whether a second person makes any difference to what they produce. It is a statement about the shape a transmission takes, and it can be settled by reading, which is why it stands here and not at a protocol."@en .
